l and burner diameter, an increase in coflow velocity generally increases the smoke point length.\nContext: In Fig. 2 the measured smoke point lengths are plotted with respect "}, {"chunk_id": "psi:PSI-107", "score": 0.0157, "text": "Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment (SPICE) (PSI-107), flight experiment. Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment (SPICE) Hardware: SPICE. Fuel: Gas jets: CH4, C2H4, propane, propylene. Key conditions: Burner"}, {"chunk_id": "card:F-P2-1d7c4ea4-1-1", "score": 0.0156, "text": "Smoke points of nonbuoyant coflowing flames decrease with increasing burner diameter.\nContext: Figure 2 reveals that smoke points of nonbuoyant coflowing flames decrease with increasing burner diamete"}, {"chunk_id": "card:F-P2-7b7541f3-1-2", "score": 0.0153, "text": "The present smoke-point lengths scale with d^(-0.91) u_air^(0.41).\nContext: The major conclusions are as follows:\nEvidence: On the ISS, the authors report."}, {"chunk_id": "card:F-P2-c139e0ce-2-2", "score": 0.0146, "text": "These two effects can be explained qualitatively by considering residence times; increased fuel and/or coflow velocities yield increased smoke point lengths.\nContext: The major conclusions are as foll"}], "composer": {"provider": "gemini", "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "key": "gemini#3", "fallback_reason": "", "sentences": []}, "outcome": "not_found", "latency_ms": {"scope": 0, "search": 2088, "search_embed": 300, "search_db": 132, "search_vector": 131, "search_keyword": 39, "search_rerank": 1657, "compose": 773, "total": 2861}, "keys": {"embed": "cohere#3", "composer": "gemini#3"}, "reason": "the composer found no answer in the excerpts", "path": "rag", "reranker": "rrf (rerank unavailable)", "outcome_reason": "the composer found no answer in the excerpts"}
